<h2 id=3D"6">OUPS meetup october 2026</h2>
<div class=3D"outline-text-2" id=3D"text-6">
<p>
Archive: <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/oups-meetup-november-2026/1=
8610/1">https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/oups-meetup-november-2026/18610/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-orgdf71785" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"orgdf71785">ancolie announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-orgdf71785">
<p>
The next OUPS meetup will take place on <b>Tuesday, 3rd of November</b> 202=
6. It will start at <b>6:30pm</b> at the <b>4 place Jussieu</b> in Paris. I=
t will be in the in the <b>amphitheater 45A</b>. There is a <a href=3D"http=
s://sciences.sorbonne-universite.fr/vie-de-campus-sciences/accueil-vie-prat=
ique/plan-du-campus">map of the buildings</a>.
</p>

<p>
Please, <b><a href=3D"https://www.meetup.com/fr-FR/ocaml-paris/events/31694=
5503">register on meetup</a></b> as soon as possible to let us know how man=
y pizza we should order.
</p>

<p>
For more details, you may check the <a href=3D"https://oups.frama.io">OUPS=
=E2=80=99 website </a>.
</p>

<p>
Moreover, we=E2=80=99d like to announce that the organizing team moved to t=
he <a href=3D"https://ocaml.zulipchat.com">OCaml Zulip</a>. Feel free to co=
ntact us there if you=E2=80=99d like to suggest talks.
</p>

<p>
&mdash;
</p>

<p>
This time we=E2=80=99ll have the following talks:
</p>

<p>
<b>Compiling FFI-heavy OCaml to WebAssembly: An Experience Report on MOPSA =
&ndash; R=C3=A9da Boudrouss</b>
</p>

<p>
Large OCaml applications often rely on native C and C++ libraries through t=
he OCaml FFI, making them difficult to run in the browser. Existing browser=
 backends compile OCaml to JavaScript or WebAssembly, but leave the native =
dependency stack behind.
</p>

<p>
In this presentation, we explore a different approach: compiling the OCaml =
runtime together with the complete native dependency stack to WebAssembly u=
sing Emscripten, and running the original OCaml bytecode on top of it. FFI =
calls are resolved through a static primitive table, without modifying exis=
ting FFI code or CamlIDL-generated bindings.
</p>

<p>
We demonstrate this approach on MOPSA, a static analyzer combining OCaml wi=
th LLVM/Clang, GMP, MPFR, Apron, and CamlIDL, and show how the complete app=
lication can run directly in a web browser.
</p>

<p>
<b>Designing a Set-Theoretic Type Library in OCaml &ndash; Micka=C3=ABl Lau=
rent</b>
</p>

<p>
In this talk, I will share my experience using OCaml to design a library fo=
r set-theoretic types.
Set-theoretic types are difficult to define because they consist of several=
 recursive components and layers.
Furthermore, the termination of certain operations on recursive types depen=
ds on a hash-consing system operating at various levels.
I will discuss the challenges and advantages of using OCaml, such as the di=
fficulty of making some recursive modules typecheck and how we used algebra=
ic effects to implement our hash-consing system.
</p>

<p>
&mdash;
</p>

<p>
After the talks there will be some pizzas offered by the <a href=3D"https:/=
/ocaml-sf.org">OCaml Software Foundation</a> and later on we=E2=80=99ll mov=
e to a pub nearby as usual.
</p>
</div>
