<div id=3D"outline-container-7" class=3D"outline-2">
<h2 id=3D"7">faker 0.1.1: fake (but realistic) data, seed-compatible with f=
aker-js</h2>
<div class=3D"outline-text-2" id=3D"text-7">
<p>
Archive: <a href=3D"https://discuss.ocaml.org/t/ann-faker-0-1-1-fake-but-re=
alistic-data-seed-compatible-with-faker-js/18611/1">https://discuss.ocaml.o=
rg/t/ann-faker-0-1-1-fake-but-realistic-data-seed-compatible-with-faker-js/=
18611/1</a>
</p>
</div>
<div id=3D"outline-container-org2ada424" class=3D"outline-3">
<h3 id=3D"org2ada424">Bartek Teodorczyk announced</h3>
<div class=3D"outline-text-3" id=3D"text-org2ada424">
<p>
I'm happy to announce <a href=3D"https://ocaml.org/p/faker">faker</a>, a li=
brary for generating fake but realistic data: names, addresses, emails, pho=
ne numbers, companies, dates, finance data, lorem ipsum and more, in 77 loc=
ales.
</p>

<pre class=3D"example" id=3D"orgde87630">
opam install faker
</pre>

<p>
As far as I could find, OCaml didn't have a faker-style library. Property-t=
esting generators like QCheck give you random values, but not plausible, lo=
calised fixtures for demos, seeding a development database or snapshot test=
s.
</p>

<p>
faker is a port of <a href=3D"https://github.com/faker-js/faker">@faker-js/=
faker</a> 10.6.0, with <b><b>seed-for-seed parity</b></b>: for the same see=
d, every method returns exactly what faker-js returns. It uses the same Mer=
senneTwister19937 generator and the same algorithms, so an OCaml service an=
d a JS/TS service can share fixtures.
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-ocaml"><code><span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weig=
ht: bold;">let</span> () =3D
  <span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">f</span> =3D <span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weigh=
t: bold;">Faker.</span>create <span style=3D"color: #444fcf; font-weight: b=
old;">~seed</span>:42 () <span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;"=
>in</span>
  print_endline (<span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.P=
erson.</span>full_name f);
  print_endline (<span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.I=
nternet.</span>email f);
  print_endline (<span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.L=
ocation.</span>city f);
  print_endline
    (<span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.Helpers.</spa=
n>fake <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"{{person.firstName}} works as a {{p=
erson.jobTitle}}"</span> f);
  <span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">de</span> =3D <span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weig=
ht: bold;">Faker.Locales.De.</span>faker () <span style=3D"color: #242521; =
font-weight: bold;">in</span>
  <span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.</span>seed de 4=
2;
  print_endline (<span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.P=
erson.</span>full_name de);
  <span style=3D"color: #242521; font-weight: bold;">let</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">ja</span> =3D <span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weig=
ht: bold;">Faker.Locales.Ja.</span>faker () <span style=3D"color: #242521; =
font-weight: bold;">in</span>
  <span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.</span>seed ja 4=
2;
  print_endline (<span style=3D"color: #557400; font-weight: bold;">Faker.P=
erson.</span>full_name ja)
</code></pre>
</div>

<pre class=3D"example" id=3D"org04b94bc">
Nikita Crist
Moises.Sipes-Effertz18@gmail.com
Lolaville
Jeannette works as a Forward Implementation Analyst
Melinda Dragu
=E5=90=89=E5=B7=9D =E7=B5=90=E8=A1=A3
</pre>

<p>
The equivalent faker-js code prints the same six lines.
</p>

<p>
<b>What's in it</b>
</p>

<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li>All 26 faker-js modules (<code>Faker.Person</code>, <code>Faker.Interne=
t</code>, <code>Faker.Location</code>, <code>Faker.Date</code>, <code>Faker=
.Finance</code>, <code>Faker.Commerce</code>, &hellip;) and all 77 locales.=
</li>
<li>The API mirrors faker-js, so the <a href=3D"https://fakerjs.dev/api/">f=
aker-js API reference</a> documents every method. The faker instance is alw=
ays the last argument, and options are optional labelled arguments: <code>f=
aker.number.int({ min: 1, max: 10 })</code> becomes <code>Faker.Number.int =
~min:1 ~max:10 f</code>.</li>
<li>No dependencies beyond the stdlib and <code>unix</code>. Locales are li=
nked on demand, so an English-only program doesn't carry the other 76.</li>
</ul>

<p>
<b>How parity is checked</b>
</p>

<p>
<code>dune test</code> compares the OCaml output with fixtures generated by=
 running faker-js itself: 3 seeds for every case, plus a sweep of every met=
hod in every locale (about 72,000 checks). Getting them all to pass meant s=
olving a few problems that may interest people here:
</p>

<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li><b>FMA contraction.</b> On arm64, the OCaml compiler fuses <code>a *. b=
 +. c</code> into a single fused multiply-add, which rounds differently fro=
m V8. The port uses an explicit multiply where it matters.</li>
<li><b>Evaluation order.</b> OCaml evaluates function arguments and record =
fields right to left, so every random draw is bound with <code>let</code> i=
n faker-js's order.</li>
<li><b>UTF-16 semantics.</b> JS string lengths, case mapping and NFKD norma=
lisation are emulated on UTF-8 strings, with tables generated from Node.</l=
i>
<li><b>Floating-point maths.</b> <code>location.nearbyGPSCoordinate</code> =
uses a port of V8's fdlibm <code>sin~/~cos</code>. The one known platform d=
ependence is <code>Math.pow</code> in the exponential distributor: Node cal=
ls the system libm, so faker-js itself rounds the last bit differently on F=
reeBSD. opam-repo-ci found this, and 0.1.1 is the fix.</li>
</ul>

<p>
The porting notes are in <a href=3D"https://github.com/barteo/faker-ocaml/b=
lob/main/docs/PORTING.md">docs/PORTING.md</a>, and the remaining difference=
s are listed in the <a href=3D"https://github.com/barteo/faker-ocaml#readme=
">README</a> under "Known differences".
</p>

<p>
<b>Links</b>
</p>

<ul class=3D"org-ul">
<li>Package and API docs: <a href=3D"https://ocaml.org/p/faker">https://oca=
ml.org/p/faker</a></li>
<li>Source: <a href=3D"https://github.com/barteo/faker-ocaml">https://githu=
b.com/barteo/faker-ocaml</a></li>
<li>Issues: <a href=3D"https://github.com/barteo/faker-ocaml/issues">https:=
//github.com/barteo/faker-ocaml/issues</a></li>
</ul>

<p>
All the data and algorithms come from faker-js, so many thanks to the faker=
-js team and contributors. Feedback and bug reports are very welcome, espec=
ially any case where the output differs from faker-js.
</p>
